A||N||48||NOS3800||Post 1/4 times here||||10||
Z||000000||NOS3800||07-11-2001||11:28 PM||||I am interested in everyones quarter mile times and what set up you used to get that time. I like to see what the different ways everyone goes about getting down the track in the fastest manner. I am new to the list and already see some VERY interesting setups, especially the turbos and NOS cars. Here's mine (you probably already know)<P>Normally Aspirated 3800 Series II<BR>4T60E auto<BR>75 shot ZEX NOS dry System<BR>GT body style<P>best so far- 13.58@ 99 mph<P>------------------<BR>Purple 1988 GT<BR>NOS injected 3800 Series II V6<BR>Best ET(75 shot)-13.58@99.08 mph<BR>w/o NOS 14.86@92.06mph||||reg||10
Z||000001||banditbalz||07-11-2001||11:54 PM||||15.2 at 90 mp/h <P>no cat, no NOS, no B.S.<P>stock 4 speed muncie, stock 2.8 <BR>_<P> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/biglaunch_3.jpg"> <BR>_<BR> <A HREF="http://www.geocities.com/banditbalz/" TARGET=_blank>www.geocities.com/banditbalz/</A> <BR><P>------------------<BR>Silver 86 GT<BR>completely stock,<BR>completely fast!||||reg||12
Z||000002||NOS3800||07-12-2001||12:06 AM||||GEEZ BAndit, looks like your wheels are almost off the ground, what rpms you spinning when you launch and what are your 60 ft times like? mine are 2.2 with out spray, 1.8 with. I must say, that is a DAMN good time for a stock car. The fastest I had ever seen was a 15.6 ran by a stock 86 GT. Manual tranny too. <P>------------------<BR>Purple 1988 GT<BR>NOS injected 3800 Series II V6<BR>Best ET(75 shot)-13.58@99.08 mph<BR>w/o NOS 14.86@92.06mph||||reg||1
Z||000003||Terrybogin||07-12-2001||12:24 AM||||Have not taken it to the track yet, will G-tech do?||||reg||1
Z||000004||banditbalz||07-12-2001||12:32 AM||||I launch at 3000 rpm and hold to redline in both first and second. third gear I shift earlier, usually around 5800-6000. My best 60 foot was a 1.9 I consistantly pull 2.0 to 2.1<BR>just looking over a few time slips here, my 1/8th all seem to be 9.8<BR>yeah, it's funny, but the steering gets real light at launch. I run on stock size street tires (Eagle GT+4). <BR>Suspension is stiffer now, I just had Eibach springs, KYB shocks and GM performance struts installed so I would guess my launches will suffer.<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Untitled_2.jpg"> <BR><P>------------------<BR>Silver 86 GT<BR>completely stock,<BR>completely fast!||||reg||14
Z||000005||Hartz||07-12-2001||09:11 AM||||13.34 1/4 mile using a mostly stock 350 TPI motor from a '89 Trans Am GTA (headers, 3 inch exhaust, and cold air intake), mated to a 5 speed Getrag.  This on cheap P215/60/15 street tires.<P>Hartz<BR>'84 SC 5.7L V8<BR>'85 SE 3.1L V6 Turbo||||reg||1
Z||000006||KissMySSFiero||07-12-2001||12:54 PM||||my best was 15.8.  and was running consistant 15.9's.  i have a getrag and stock 6(at the time)  only thing was i had no cat.  car has over 160k miles.  ive added a cold air intake now so i need to get back out to the track.  =Þ<P>------------------<BR>JOEJOE Aol:SSFiero <P>88FORMULA<BR>      <A HREF="mailto:SSFiero@Aol.com">SSFiero@Aol.com</A>       <P>      <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/FORUMSIG1.jpg">           <BR>"Kids in the backseat cause accidents,  accidents in the backseat caused me!!"||||reg||1
Z||000007||Alex4mula||07-12-2001||04:18 PM||||I tried my new automatic Formula last night but only 0-60 on G-Tech. Bought it with 117K miles, changed plugs($12), wires ($18), cleaned distributor ($0!) and added ATF (one quart). <BR>Best time was 8.51s 0-60 with just smashing the pedal (no preload) at launch. I was amaze! It do feels slow at start.<BR>Which launch techniques you use on autos? ||||reg||1
Z||000008||Mach10||07-12-2001||05:08 PM||||Just mash and go, man. I keep it in 1-low on the start, and rev to 5300rpm, that way, when the TH-125_Snail shifts, it's not too far below your powerband. You can feel the tires ALMOST slip with that kind of shift. The rest I leave up to the valve bodies  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><BR>No times, yet.||||reg||1
Z||000009||California Kid||07-12-2001||06:37 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by NOS3800:<BR><B>I am interested in everyones quarter mile times and what set up you used to get that time. I like to see what the different ways everyone goes about getting down the track in the fastest manner. I am new to the list and already see some VERY interesting setups, especially the turbos and NOS cars. Here's mine (you probably already know)<P>Normally Aspirated 3800 Series II<BR>4T60E auto<BR>75 shot ZEX NOS dry System<BR>GT body style<P>best so far- 13.58@ 99 mph<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Day at strip resulted in best of 12.26 @ 136 mph.  This time resulted from dumping the clutch at 3,500 on the first yellow, lots of tire spin from BFG r-TA's (did not red light). Car is really setup for road course racing, for pictures see post (picture of car)by California Kid this date. Just wanted to see what it would do in quarter, on street tires.<BR>Tom||||reg||1
Z||000010||Jay||07-12-2001||06:57 PM||||My best time at the track, It was raining and wet was 13.8 @ 104 mph.  My speedo (digital gauge) on the street in good conditions is telling me 13.4 I'll be taking it to the track again to confirm, but it deffinately feels faster with the resonators cut out.  Oh yeah, 1986 GT with a 4.6L Northstar engine, automatic tranny, true duel exhaust (3") with 2 catalytics. <P>Jay<BR>1986 Northstar GT <A HREF="http://www.members.home.net/jaygrande" TARGET=_blank>www.members.home.net/jaygrande</A> <P>"She's fast enough for you old man, what's the cargo?"-Han Solo||||reg||1
Z||000011||NOS3800||07-12-2001||09:12 PM||||When I had the 2.8 mated to the T-125, the best way to launch is mash down on the brake pedal with your left foot once you stage and push the gas with your right foot a bring up the rpm's to around 2 grand, or whatever your brakes allow.<BR>When you are ready to leave, mash the gas and release the brake, may help you pick up two tenths.<BR><P>------------------<BR>Purple 1988 GT<BR>NOS injected 3800 Series II V6<BR>Best ET(75 shot)-13.58@99.08 mph<BR>w/o NOS 14.86@92.06mph||||reg||1
Z||000012||Alex4mula||07-13-2001||08:32 AM||||NOS; I'll try that next time. Tks.||||reg||1
Z||000013||BBBAD GT||07-13-2001||08:58 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by California Kid:<BR><B> Day at strip resulted in best of 12.26 @ 136 mph.  </B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>136 mph?!?!  Are you sure that wasn't 106 or maybe 116 mph?  <BR>If that 136 mph is true, that's high nine/low ten second power.  I'm curious how the rest of the chassis/drivetrain is taking that power, and is that power NA or N2O assisted?  Yikes!<P>------------------<BR>        <IMG SRC="http://scribers.midwest.net/bbbad50/V8Fiero/V8sig.jpg">        <P><A HREF="http://home.att.net/~nomercy5.0/V8Specs.htm" TARGET=_blank>BBBAD V8 For Sale</A><P><A HREF="http://scribers.midwest.net/bbbad50/" TARGET=_blank>BBBAD 5.0</A>||||reg||1
Z||000014||Black88GT||07-13-2001||09:58 AM||||BBBAD, I just noticed that also.  I was in the chat room last night talking about that and I thought wait a minute, 136 mph?! No way!  Gotta see the slips....<P>bandit, is that constant 15.2s?<p>[This message has been edited by Black88GT (edited 07-13-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000015||California Kid||07-13-2001||10:40 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by BBBAD GT:<BR><B> 136 mph?!?!  Are you sure that wasn't 106 or maybe 116 mph?  <BR>If that 136 mph is true, that's high nine/low ten second power.  I'm curious how the rest of the chassis/drivetrain is taking that power, and is that power NA or N2O assisted?  Yikes!<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><BR>I know the mph appears to be too high, most people refuse to believe it, but that's what was on the Time Slip. Most of my runs that day were slower runs (high 12's minus about 10-15 mph), just trying to figure out how to launch the car, due to tire spin. Rear tires are BFG T/A Rad. 245X50X16, stock 5spd with double disk QuarterMaster Clutch, Engine hp conservative @400 (running twin 52mm Lingenfelter throttle body, but indications are I should be running the 58mm)no NO2, and custom burned engine chip by Meana Streets in CA. All I can say is that this quick run resulted from a "hole shot" start on the first yellow (knew I was playing in the red light zone)and the reaction time was .02 from perfect, weather was cool about 65 degrees.<P>The car handles the hp real good, if you aren't racing everybody and their brother. The two seasons I was pushing it real hard, I would wipe out the "short shaft" Mark Williams Racing Axle and inboard CV joint about every 6,000 miles. After two failures I became allot more selective on who I would run (or show off for). The QuarterMaster Double Disk Clutch Setup uses two 7.5 clutch disks with metal floater plate in between, life approx. 10,0000 miles, and is the best setup found to work in this car (other hp clutches failed in under 3,000 miles).<P>I don't drag race this car. Bottom line is that this car and every other Fiero was never intended to be a "Drag Race" car, the drive train is not designed to take "brutal launches".  What the Fiero's are excellent at is road coarse racing, which is what my car is setup for. Trip to the quarter mile was curiousity, and I don't plan on returning.<P>Tom<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000016||BBBAD GT||07-13-2001||11:29 AM||||I must say, if the timeslip showed it, then I'm amazed that your NA motor is producing 600 hp if you weigh 3000lbs (calculated).  It wouldn't matter at the strip if your reaction time is perfect or sleeping, the mph is measured as an average of the finish line mph and about 20 feet before.  Ever been to a dyno?  Any more specs on your motor?<P>BTW, I wouldn't go back to the strip if you are turning 136+ mph, because they would say your car is "9 second capable" based on the mph and hold you to all the safety requirements (roll cage, sfe scattershield/clutch/flywheel, harness, window net, etc...).  I've seen it done.<P>I really can't think of any small block car I know of that could turn 136 mph at 3000lbs NA with fuel injection that could be driven on the street with pump gas, let alone a Fiero.  Yours is the first.||||reg||1
Z||000017||California Kid||07-13-2001||01:06 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by BBBAD GT:<BR><B>I must say, if the timeslip showed it, then I'm amazed that your NA motor is producing 600 hp if you weigh 3000lbs (calculated).  It wouldn't matter at the strip if your reaction time is perfect or sleeping, the mph is measured as an average of the finish line mph and about 20 feet before.  Ever been to a dyno?  Any more specs on your motor?<P>BTW, I wouldn't go back to the strip if you are turning 136+ mph, because they would say your car is "9 second capable" based on the mph and hold you to all the safety requirements (roll cage, sfe scattershield/clutch/flywheel, harness, window net, etc...).  I've seen it done.<P>I really can't think of any small block car I know of that could turn 136 mph at 3000lbs NA with fuel injection that could be driven on the street with pump gas, let alone a Fiero.  Yours is the first.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Sorry guys, don't have allot of hard core facts. Do know that the car weighed in at 2910 with a full tank of gas after conversion was done, and track runs were made with 1/4 or less tank of gas. Also, the only time is was on the dyno was just after the conversion in '93 when it yielded 330 hp @ rear tires (and it was running alittle rich, as injectors were modified by some specialist named "Jones" in CA). This was before porting the alum heads, lower intake, adding SLP runners (port matched), plenum porting, and adding the twin 52mm throttle body. <P>The engine is a highly modified L98 that came out of a 91 Vette, worked over by well know "speed nuts" in CA, I just freed up the breathing. Some of the core engine parts are TPI Super Profile Cam, Crower 1.6:1 roller rockers, Sanderson 100cc hearders into 3" collector to modified FlowMaster Muffler.<P>Needless to say, I couldn't be happier with the car and I'm not interested in spending $500 for dyno time to get a spec sheet. Hope this satisfies some of your curiousity.<BR>Tom <P>||||reg||1
Z||000018||1FST2M6||07-13-2001||01:27 PM||||$500 for a dyno run.. it's $65 at the most... we have a Dynojet and i'm sure if you find a dyno center near you i could call them and get a few bucks knocked off. <BR> <A HREF="http://www.dynojet.com" TARGET=_blank>www.dynojet.com</A>  look in the locater. for true real time data aquisition it's only $140 an hour... $500... uum  ...<P><BR>oh 2.8 nitrous 86 SE headers, DOT slicks 13.6@103. (july 30, 1999.) <P>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Fierotime.jpg">  <BR>on 14" street tires earlier in the day. i can't find the jpg of the 13.6 run... 164hp 253lbft <P>using the NHRA calculations.. this should have been a 13.8 car on plain tires..  1/8th mile time x 1.54= 1/4 time estimate. <P>my current GT not on nitrous runs 9.8 in the 1/8th. (15.0 estimate 1/4) 9.831@67mph<P> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/1fst9.jpg"> <P>(CLIFF!!!   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"> i miss being able to go searching in the picture archives, i'm missing lots of cool pics... i know it's not your fault others are lame, but i still miss it)<P><p>[This message has been edited by 1FST2M6 (edited 07-13-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000019||banditbalz||07-13-2001||06:57 PM||||"bandit, is that constant 15.2s?"<P>Black88GT, no, that was a one off. Never seen it again. I can run 15.3-15.4 all day long. I now have a new clutch, tranny and different tires and feel confident that I could better a 15.2<P>what surprises me is my 90 mph trap speed. That number is a strong indication of horsepower<P> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/xxx.jpg"> <BR>_ <A HREF="http://www.geocities.com/banditbalz/" TARGET=_blank>www.geocities.com/banditbalz/</A> <P><P>------------------<BR>Silver 86 GT<BR>completely stock,<BR>completely fast!||||reg||12
Z||000020||Jay||07-13-2001||07:12 PM||||So Bandit when are we all gonna go to the strip again?<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/cayuga1.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000021||banditbalz||07-13-2001||07:22 PM||||Jay, had a great time watching you do a 1320 foot burnout! We have to do it again soon! Let's wait for SlammedFiero to get his NOS setup (this weekend) and Grinthock to get his ****  togethor and we will all head out agian. Maybe we can get Bob to bring the 3.8 s/c so you have a little, albeit slower, competition...<P>here's a pic of my GT laying the smack down on a new Miata.<BR>_<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/smack.jpg"> <P>_ <A HREF="http://www.geocities.com/banditbalz/" TARGET=_blank>www.geocities.com/banditbalz/</A> <P><P>------------------<BR>Silver 86 GT<BR>completely stock,<BR>completely fast!||||reg||14
Z||000022||pherder||07-13-2001||08:02 PM||||9.98 @ 135.52<P>350 pound bike ... 150 pound rider.<P>1979!<P>750 H2 Kawasaki Triple.<P>You didn't specify in a Fiero!!!<P> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>Paul||||reg||6
Z||000023||NOS3800||07-14-2001||12:12 PM||||Ok, I have alot to say. FIRST off, I noticed the 136 mph too. That is impossible. The sport bike above ran a 9 sec at 136 and it weighs, what 500 pounds? A bird, mouse, rabbit or a little gnome must have tripped the lights on your run, California Kid, its been known to happen. But the fact that you say you will NEVER take back to the drags makes me believe you do not want anyone to see what it really runs. 333 HP at the wheels will not net a 136 trap speed.<BR>Jay, what the hell are you racing in that pic? A Skylark? MAN, some people will take anything to the track these days. I had to race a four door Escort last nite( with GTR badges). He wasn't even NOS worthy. I ran a 14.9 at 91 mph and he ran, get this, a 23.99 at 70 mph. He had his sweet girlfriend with him too. What does she see in him???<BR>FAST86GT, those are great times with spray. Its a basically stock 2.8 with headers? What shot are you using? I run a 75 shot and you are close to my times. How long does your bottle last? I am only getting maybe 10 runs out of my 10lb bottle.<BR>Some said a 3.8 SC motor will not compete with a NORTHSTAR, but I beg to differ. My brother has one with a 3.4 Pulley in his 85 and he is consistly getting 13.2-13.3 at 100mph out of it. He even gets a great deal of tire spin. With one more pulley change he could be in the high twelves. Oh yeah, these times are with the 4T65E AUTOMATIC!<P>------------------<BR>Purple 1988 GT<BR>NOS injected 3800 Series II V6<BR>Best ET(75 shot)-13.58@99.08 mph<BR>w/o NOS 14.86@92.06mph||||reg||12
Z||000024||NOS3800||07-14-2001||12:19 PM||||I am sorry, I meant to say 1FST2M6, not FAST86GT. Sorry for any confusion.<P>------------------<BR>Purple 1988 GT<BR>NOS injected 3800 Series II V6<BR>Best ET(75 shot)-13.58@99.08 mph<BR>w/o NOS 14.86@92.06mph||||reg||1
Z||000025||grinthock||07-14-2001||12:33 PM||||my 1985 GT with pretty worn suspension in the rear (and CRAP tires) was running 15.6 as my one off best, and 15.7-15.8 for normal.<P>My 1985 Automagic (with some upgrades)<BR>has run 16.5 but I had a traction issue because it was wet that day, NOS is on the forefront for the car, so I will let everyone know.||||reg||1
Z||000026||Jay||07-14-2001||05:35 PM||||NOS3800 I know what you mean, people will race in any car.  I'll agree with you that a 3800SC will give a N* a run for the money.  There is a guy here in Toronto with a 3800SC that can barely hit 14.0 with NOS!  But I have to believe that it is the driver.  There is a 2.8Turbo intercooled that is running identical times as I am in the N*.  A great deal has to do with the driver and conditions.  There is no substitute for experience (most of the time anyway.)<P>Jay<BR>1986 Northstar GT <A HREF="http://www.members.home.net/jaygrande" TARGET=_blank>www.members.home.net/jaygrande</A> ||||reg||1
Z||000027||grinthock||07-14-2001||05:39 PM||||JAY!  How's it going -- be sure to read my "Didn't make it to MI" thread....<P><BR>The best street race I EVER saw (and jay you were there I think!)<P>Was Between the FieroShop's 2.8 Turbo Intercooled car and the Ferrari 355 with the 3.8SC Conv by Ramsey.<P>It was going westbound after leaving Happy Greek on Steeles near 400... Right at the light, some RICER tried to keep up and everyone passed him!<P>It TOTALLY looked like a scene from the Fast and The Furious Fiero's<p>[This message has been edited by grinthock (edited 07-14-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000028||88formula||07-14-2001||06:00 PM||||Who won the race, the turbo 2.8 or the 3800SC car?<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/88formula_logo_1.JPG"> ||||reg||1
Z||000029||California Kid||07-14-2001||09:33 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by NOS3800:<BR><B>Ok, I have alot to say. FIRST off, I noticed the 136 mph too. That is impossible. The sport bike above ran a 9 sec at 136 and it weighs, what 500 pounds? A bird, mouse, rabbit or a little gnome must have tripped the lights on your run, California Kid, its been known to happen. But the fact that you say you will NEVER take back to the drags makes me believe you do not want anyone to see what it really runs. 333 HP at the wheels will not net a 136 trap speed.<P><BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><BR>In response to NOS3800, I also have an '85 Ninja ZX900 with GPZ modified engine that turns the quarter in 9.65 @ 137 mph, so I know what you're getting at.<P>The trap speed on the car may have been a fluke, who knows ?? I just posted what the slip had said, and no I don't have it.<P>I didn't state that the car was running 333 HP at the rear wheels when the run was made, that was the HP dyno rating before modifications were made to the upper engine, and 48mm twin throttle body changed to twin 52mm, plus MSD & distributor mods.<P>I won't go back to the strip because I'm already pushing the limits of the transmission, Mark Williams Alloy Racing Axles (broke two in the last two years, which aren't cheap), and the CV Joints (also broke those when the axles sheared). The Car will already beat a Viper GTS with 50 extra horse from 0 to 150 and you're exactly right, I don't want anyone to know just what the car will do, it just makes it all that much more fun!!! If I wanted to get real serious about Drag Racing (9's to 11's), I wouldn't use the transverse setup. See "Burnouts" post on how the transmissions like it. <P>||||reg||1
Z||000030||BBBAD GT||07-15-2001||09:36 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by California Kid:<BR><B> Mark Williams Alloy Racing Axles (broke two in the last two years, which aren't cheap), and the CV Joints (also broke those when the axles sheared). </B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I've heard a rumor that Archie will replace your <B>STOCK</B> axles if they break.  It looks like you are breaking aftermarket axles.....<BR><P>------------------<BR>         <IMG SRC="http://scribers.midwest.net/bbbad50/V8Fiero/V8sig.jpg">         <P><A HREF="http://home.att.net/~nomercy5.0/V8Specs.htm" TARGET=_blank>BBBAD V8 For Sale *SOLD*</A><P><A HREF="http://scribers.midwest.net/bbbad50/" TARGET=_blank>BBBAD 5.0</A>||||reg||1
