Z||000032||jake1852||07-15-2001||10:18 AM||||hey BBad GT - how does your v8fiero do up against your 5.0?<P>------------------<BR>- Unfinished ferrari kit, custom made myself<BR>-87 duke (just got it, its fast, and i dont know why yet!)<BR>- 87 GT<BR>- Tc Maserati (daily drive)||||reg||1
Z||000033||BBBAD GT||07-15-2001||11:51 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by jake1852:<BR><B>hey BBad GT - how does your v8fiero do up against your 5.0?<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>LOL, my Blown 5.0 will eat the V8 Fiero's breakfast, and then steal his milk money.  But seriously, the 5.0 is a purpose built car that its only job is to smear your face with acceleration, period.  The Fiero, while it does have some extra balls, shines when you pass a bunch of high school kids and their jaws drop, with the looks and sound (the 5.0 gets similar attention, with its blower whine and lumpy cam).  The two actually complement each other, satifying my needs whatever mood I'm in.  But sadly, 4 cars is one too many (did I say that?) and the Fiero had to go.  ||||reg||1
Z||000034||Formula88||07-15-2001||12:01 PM||||  <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Archie:<BR><B> Yeah, It appears that he has a Zumwalt kit or a copy there-of.<P>Archie</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>As awesome California Kids V8 swap is, what a great advertisement for Archie!   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>Has anyone ever broken an axle in an Archie swap?  Blown trannys, yes, but not axles.  <p>[This message has been edited by Formula88 (edited 07-15-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000035||Slammed Fiero||07-15-2001||12:15 PM||||BanditBalz:<P>Got the NOS in , I arrived home at 11:00 worked til 4 in the morning. I have one little vacum hose to hook up and sort out , but thats it. All the wires are hidden , I decided to mount the bottle in the truck , as I don't want to drop $100 I don't have on a NOS line.<P><BR>Still working on the brakes , I plan to take it to the strip for the first time in the begining of August. I was mad last night , went to a bar with some friends downtown. Saw so many ricers , tiburons , old calpped out 305 IROCs. I could have had some fun  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"><P>Oh well..also got my dash back together wit the white face gauges...looks good!<P><P>------------------<BR>Jonathan McCreery<BR>86 GT 4spd<BR>Koni's , Coilovers, Big Bars , 17" Trmotorsports Typhoons , 11.25" vented discs, Wilwood 4 Piston Calipers , Pirelli P Zero's, Short Shift, No cat , Cold air induction , Geni Stainless tips, White face gauges , Momo shift , momo pedals, White face Gauges , 70Hp Shot of NOS Autocrossed , Tracked , Dragged? (soon) abused.||||reg||1
Z||000036||California Kid||07-15-2001||02:19 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Formula88:<BR><B>  As awesome California Kids V8 swap is, what a great advertisement for Archie!    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>Has anyone ever broken an axle in an Archie swap?  Blown trannys, yes, but not axles.  <P>[This message has been edited by Formula88 (edited 07-15-2001).]</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Just so other "horse power" nuts don't get mis-lead by your comments. The failures of the CV Joints and Axles are not conversion kit related. These failures are directly related to exceeding the loads (and their 3X Safety factor,)that they were designed to take. "Mark Williams" is one of the top names in Drag Race Axles and that includes Funny Car and AA Fuel. Thinking his axles might be a little too hard, I had a stock short shaft shortened for my application and ran it for awhile, the shaft twisted and bent before shearing off.<P>The weak link in my current setup is the "Short Shaft" inner CV Joint. It will only take so many hole shots before it breaks, and when it does it takes the axle out (snaps it right at the joint interface). I could reduce the loads on the joint by going to tires that didn't grip as well as the ones I'm using (problem is that they grip like hell on street pavement, where people push you to do a burnout,etc).<P>There is only one other weak link possibility that I'll investigate the next time the clutch is changed and that is the bushings on the trans case @ the outputs for the half shafts (120,000 on this trans). Have the needle bearing/seal replacements which are supposed to decrease wear in trans and stabilize the half shaft asm. I could be in a situation of harmonic vibration failure of the CV Joint. But the bottom line is that anyone with Engineering and Metallurgy experience will tell you these joints weren't designed to handle horsepower in excess of 300 HP and last very long. Secondly, why would GM put such a strong part in a 140 HP V6 package ???<P>If anyone is in the progress of a High Horse Conversion, they would be smart to do the research and have some new axles fabricated with known stronger CV Joints (ie Porsche).<P>As far as the remarks on the old "Zumalt Kit", it's the best kit for getting a true factory install look. No dis-respect intended against "ARCHIE" but even his customers (who have seen this car)are truely amazed at how well everything fits and looks.<P>This game is all about how far you want to take your toy and how big your wallet is !!!||||reg||1
Z||000037||Tweek||07-15-2001||03:21 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by California Kid:<BR><B> Day at strip resulted in best of 12.26 @ 136 mph. </B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P> <IMG SRC="http://forums.off-topic.net/images/smilies/bsflag.gif"> <BR>||||reg||1
Z||000038||Will||07-15-2001||10:16 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by California Kid:<BR><B> Just so other "horse power" nuts don't get mis-lead by your comments. The failures of the CV Joints and Axles are not conversion kit related. These failures are directly related to exceeding the loads (and their 3X Safety factor,)that they were designed to take. "Mark Williams" is one of the top names in Drag Race Axles and that includes Funny Car and AA Fuel. Thinking his axles might be a little too hard, I had a stock short shaft shortened for my application and ran it for awhile, the shaft twisted and bent before shearing off.<P>The weak link in my current setup is the "Short Shaft" inner CV Joint. It will only take so many hole shots before it breaks, and when it does it takes the axle out (snaps it right at the joint interface). I could reduce the loads on the joint by going to tires that didn't grip as well as the ones I'm using (problem is that they grip like hell on street pavement, where people push you to do a burnout,etc).<P>There is only one other weak link possibility that I'll investigate the next time the clutch is changed and that is the bushings on the trans case @ the outputs for the half shafts (120,000 on this trans). Have the needle bearing/seal replacements which are supposed to decrease wear in trans and stabilize the half shaft asm. I could be in a situation of harmonic vibration failure of the CV Joint. But the bottom line is that anyone with Engineering and Metallurgy experience will tell you these joints weren't designed to handle horsepower in excess of 300 HP and last very long. Secondly, why would GM put such a strong part in a 140 HP V6 package ???<P>If anyone is in the progress of a High Horse Conversion, they would be smart to do the research and have some new axles fabricated with known stronger CV Joints (ie Porsche).<P>As far as the remarks on the old "Zumalt Kit", it's the best kit for getting a true factory install look. No dis-respect intended against "ARCHIE" but even his customers (who have seen this car)are truely amazed at how well everything fits and looks.<P>This game is all about how far you want to take your toy and how big your wallet is !!!</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><BR>What kind of transmission are you running if you're not breaking the trans when you break axles?<BR>Maybe the increased angle caused by shortening the short axle is contributing to CV joint failure. That's a shortcoming of the Zumalt style installation<BR>Don't forget that the Zumalt method of inserting an SBC involves notching the left frame rail to clear the relocated transmission. However nice it looks, that's much worse than putting a hole in the relatively non-stressed wheel house.<P>I must say that your time slip was a fluke. The ET sounds about right, but the MPH is definitely way off. If you dynoed 333 RWHP, the mods you mentioned you did afterward are not going to put you into 135 MPH territory.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000039||BBBAD GT||07-16-2001||09:24 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by California Kid:<BR><B> This game is all about how far you want to take your toy and how big your wallet is !!!</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Amen<P>------------------<BR>         <IMG SRC="http://scribers.midwest.net/bbbad50/V8Fiero/V8sig.jpg">         <P><A HREF="http://home.att.net/~nomercy5.0/V8Specs.htm" TARGET=_blank>BBBAD V8 For Sale *SOLD*</A><P><A HREF="http://scribers.midwest.net/bbbad50/" TARGET=_blank>BBBAD 5.0</A>||||reg||1
Z||000040||1FST2M6||07-16-2001||10:38 AM||||I could lift my SEs front tires off the ground and i NEVER broke an axle... i broke clutch plates and trans input splines  (250lbft all the time 310 2-3 times a month) 2.8 on nitrous.  ||||reg||1
Z||000041||Formula88||07-16-2001||10:54 AM||||Cali Kid, no disrespect.  I love your car, but I've <B><I>never</B></I> heard of anyone breaking a stock Fiero axle in one of Archie's kit's.  And with this Forum being what it is, I'm sure someone would have posted about it.  Like I said, some blown clutches and shatters trannys, but no broken axles.  Not to put words in Archie's mouth, but if I remember his website correctly, in the entire time he's done V8 Fieros, he's never had an axle failure.  I have to think the problem isn't in the construction of the axle, but in the geometry shift you get when you move the engine to the side.  That or you just beat on your car way more than anyone else on here.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">  <P>The interesting part is that the rest of your drivetrain is holding up enough to break the axle.  What kind of tranny and clutch are you running?  If it's that strong, you may be on to something other V8 owners want to check out!||||reg||6
Z||000042||KissMySSFiero||07-16-2001||01:30 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by California Kid:<BR><B> <P>As far as the remarks on the old "Zumalt Kit", it's the best kit for getting a true factory install look. No dis-respect intended against "ARCHIE" but even his customers (who have seen this car)are truely amazed at how well everything fits and looks.<BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><BR> <BR>personally i wouldnt care if it looks good if it cant hold together.  ive only seen a few zumwalt kits in person and that was a few years ago.   but archies to me looks like a factory install AND the axles dont break under load.  <P>------------------<BR>JOEJOE Aol:SSFiero <P>88FORMULA<BR>      <A HREF="mailto:SSFiero@Aol.com">SSFiero@Aol.com</A>       <P>      <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/FORUMSIG1.jpg">           <BR>"Kids in the backseat cause accidents,  accidents in the backseat caused me!!"||||reg||1
Z||000043||California Kid||07-16-2001||08:53 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Formula88:<BR>The interesting part is that the rest of your drivetrain is holding up enough to break the axle.  What kind of tranny and clutch are you running?  If it's that strong, you may be on to something other V8 owners want to check out![/B]<HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I think it's important to understand that they're aren't many people out there running 400 plus horse power (engine or @ rear tires). <P>Secondly, I only know of one other person running the QuarterMaster Double Disc Clutch Setup (he's at 300 hp). The clutch setup is a race only application not intended for street use, there are no springs in the disc centers, solid metal. The clutch has to be loaded and unloaded very quickly as the friction material doesn't not "sliping the clutch". The reason for this setup is that it "hooks up" like you wouldn't believe (most clutches slip alittle, but you can't detect it's happening and they handle heat alot better, the hub springs also absorb some off the initial energy). You have to drive a car with and without this setup to really appreciate what I'm talking about (see post from "ARTHERD" today date under topic "Picture of Car", he know's as he's driven cars with this setup, you don't have to take my word for it).<P>Got a little of track in answering your question, but this is all valuable information. The Trans is the original 5spd Getrag that the car was manufactured with, it currently has 120,000 miles on it. The V8 conversion took place at 90,000 miles at which time the trans was examined and just a couple bearings were replaced (with stock parts) as insurance. All CV Joints are "stock" parts, the splined axles are Mark Williams Chrome Moly Alloy racing parts, the rear suspension has be lowered 1.5 inches, and rear tires are "VR" rated B F Goodrich Radial T/A's 245X50X16 (which stick like crazy on the street, but spin like crazy at the strip). That's the setup.<P>You comment about the shortened shaft changing the load angle on the CV Joint: It's only shortened 1 to 1.5 inch which doesn't change the shaft angle very much at all, and the chassis lowering compensated for it a little. I've checked the position of the axle with full suspension compression load and full unload (tire off ground)and the static position of the axle (parked) is in the center off the travel. While we're in this area let me make a comment about a post that someone make indicating that this kit was inferior, because the frame rail had to be notched for trans clearance. Yes the rail is notched, but it was also reinforced by welding in more sheet sheet (30,000 miles of very hard driving and no indications of stress in that area, I worried too.)<P>Why hasn't my transmission exploded yet??? I don't have an answer for you, I worry a little about it because of horror stories I've heard, and I know I'm pushing it way beyond what it was designed to handle (and have any durability). In fact lately I've been researching to find a better replacement for it in the event it fails down the road (which it certainly will). It could be that the short shaft inboard CV Joint is saving my butt by failing and releasing the load from the transmission.<P>To clear up things the failure mode I'm experiencing the sequence of events is that the CV Joint fails first which takes out the axle. If it was only the shaft shearing the CV Joint would be unaffected by the failure. Also, the failure has only occurred since I upped the horsepower and it takes about 6 to 8,000 miles before the failure happens. I still think it related to the Transmission Case Bronse Bushings being worn out (120,000 miles) causing the shaft not to rotate true, which is setting up a harmonic fatique failure in the CV Joint. About my driving, I drive this car "hard", it's got a V8 in it for a reason, and that's to enjoy aceleration. <P>Some of you might be wondering, who is this guy, and what's his background ??<BR>I'm 50 (young at heart, old fart), a Senior Automotive Engineer with more than 25 years experience working for GM and Chrysler Product Engineering Groups. I don't claim to be an expert, but I do know my way around a automobile pretty well. I know I'm going to have to take some "flak" from this paragraph, but just wanted you to understand I've been playing with cars for a long time.<P>Please understand that I don't feel I have a problem with my car. I'm not asking for help, it suits my purpose just fine and has exceeded all my expectations in performance and handling (I've got two Ninja's that are quicker, but prefer to drive the GT). If and when I find the gremlin I'll post a note.<P>Sorry for the long response, hope some of you find this information useful.<BR>Tom<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000044||BV MotorSports||07-16-2001||10:02 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by NOS3800:<BR><B><P>Normally Aspirated 3800 Series II<BR>4T60E auto<BR>75 shot ZEX NOS dry System<BR>GT body style<P>best so far- 13.58@ 99 mph<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><BR>Wow...you might want to recheck your N20...your MPH should be much higher!<BR>With a 75hp shot you should get more than 6mph. Sounds like you are getting about 30-40hp shot.<P>Steven<P><P>------------------<BR>87 Fiero GT 14.9/ 89mph<BR>94 Formula WS6 w/ NOS 12.4 /118mph<BR>87 Conquest TSi <BR>87 Conquest TSi 406 SBC V8!<BR>85.5 Starion ESi||||reg||4
Z||000045||NOS3800||07-17-2001||12:15 AM||||Well, without NOS it runs a 14.8-15.1 at 91-92 mph. Everyone says my car should run mid 14's but have never achieved this and I do not know why. Anyway, I do pick up 6-7 mph with the 75 shot.<P> <P>------------------<BR>Purple 1988 GT<BR>NOS injected 3800 Series II V6<BR>Best ET(75 shot)-13.58@99.08 mph<BR>w/o NOS 14.86@92.06mph||||reg||1
Z||000046||1FST2M6||07-17-2001||08:56 AM|||| <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"> solid hubs ROCK!!!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/1fst4puck.jpg">  <P>did somone say solid hub clutch... it'll hold 310lbft (at the ground under a spray) shift at near 400lbft and chirp 4th at 85+. the new one is similar just organic pads not cindered ceramic. hoping to cut down on the chatter... (set up currently owned by GT Bastard) <P>low bottle pressures.. but still fun to look at. <BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/2m6allgear.jpg">  <P><p>[This message has been edited by 1FST2M6 (edited 07-17-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000047||BBBAD GT||07-17-2001||12:25 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by California Kid:<BR><B> I think it's important to understand that they're aren't many people out there running 400 plus horse power (engine or @ rear tires). <P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><BR>My V8 Fiero origianlly had a hot 350 in it, with alum heads/lumpy cam/single plane intake, and with the munci 4 speed, would spin the DF Centerforce clutch at high RPMs (per Chris).  I agree that there are probably few people who has had that hot of an engine with a 'good' clutch that hasn't broken axles, but I'd really be curious what a high high HP motor in a Fiero would do with a 'take no prisoners/beefed up auto trans' to the axles.  Just curious.<P><BR><P>------------------<BR>         <IMG SRC="http://scribers.midwest.net/bbbad50/V8Fiero/V8sig.jpg">         <P><A HREF="http://home.att.net/~nomercy5.0/V8Specs.htm" TARGET=_blank>BBBAD V8 For Sale *SOLD*</A><P><A HREF="http://scribers.midwest.net/bbbad50/" TARGET=_blank>BBBAD 5.0</A>||||reg||1
Z||000048||California Kid||07-17-2001||05:36 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by BBBAD GT:<BR><B> <BR>I'd really be curious what a high high HP motor in a Fiero would do with a 'take no prisoners/beefed up auto trans' to the axles.  Just curious.<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Brilliant Minds Think Alike !!! My cousin from LA (Drags a '65 GTO "Butler Big Block" Alcohol Bracket Car, 9.46 et) and I attended the Jim Butler Pontiac Drag Nationals in TN on Memorial Day weekend. He would like to build a Fiero GT with a "pumped" Pontiac 389 tripower setup and 3 or 4 spd auto trans.  We spend a lot of time during the drags talking about it, and figured if you could get the trans to hold up, the car could get into the 10's easy. I've heard there are only 2-3 people in the U.S. that can rework and "beef up" the Fiero Auto trans to take this horsepower, new output shafts in trans have to be machined (larger dia., greater strenght). It would be real sweet to see someone do it, and I'm sure that person would find a few extra weak links in the process that he has to overcome.<P>I wish I had an auto trans, my engine revs so quick, you really have to have your head together to shift quick enough. It's very similiar to my Ninja ZX900, but I don't have to use the clutch on the Ninja, just tap it into the next gear.<P>Picture below is the real way to Drag Race a Fiero, saw this guy run in TN. Didn't find out exactly what engine he was running, but it looked just like the old Pontiac 421 Interceptor Engine, "pumped off coarse" (up front where it belongs on a drag car). He "blew off" 22 entries over Sat. & Sun. racing to win overall in his class.<P> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Altered_Fiero.gif"> <BR>||||reg||3
